<UserControl
    x:Class="ClockWall.OpenworkedFace"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
    xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
    mc:Ignorable="d">

    <!--
        An openworked mechanical face: a 640px watch with the escapement running
        in an opening at six.

        WHY THIS IS ALL IMAGES. It was shapes, twice, and looked like a patent
        drawing both times. Metal does not read as metal because of its colour -
        it reads as metal because of what it REFLECTS, and because every
        machined edge carries a polished chamfer that catches a line of light.
        Neither is expressible as a SolidColorBrush, so no amount of correcting
        the geometry was ever going to fix it. The parts are modelled as solids,
        lit by a measured photographic studio, and rendered offline.

        THE FRAME IS THE POINT. Every asset is a render from an orthographic
        camera framed exactly to this control's coordinate space. So each one is
        placed at (0,0) at native size and rotated about its own centre
        expressed in the SAME coordinates the Python that generated it used.
        There is no fitting, no offset table, and moving a part in the geometry
        needs no change here.

        PAINT ORDER IS Z ORDER. A stack of Images can only express a painter's
        algorithm, so the order below is the order the parts sit in: plate,
        train, escapement, fork, spring, balance, the cock bridging over the
        lot, then the dial and case above all of it, then the hands. The cock
        has to be its own layer rather than part of the plate, or the balance is
        drawn over its own bridge.

        EACH MOVING PART CARRIES ITS OWN SHADOW. The renders put the rotating
        groups over an invisible shadow catcher, so a wheel's shadow is inside
        the wheel's own image and turns with it. Baking those shadows into the
        static plate instead left them pointing the same way all day while the
        wheels spun.

        ONE CLOCK DRIVES ALL OF IT. Every RotateTransform below is written from
        a single Caliber.Read on a single frame. Nothing here is a Storyboard,
        and that is deliberate: an animation on its own clock cannot stay in
        phase with an escapement on the wall clock, and the phase is the entire
        mechanical content of a lever.

        To regenerate: run tools/export_profiles.py, then tools/dial_render.py,
        then tools/blender_face.py under Blender in background mode. Materials
        are settled first on tools/blender_swatch.py, which renders crops of the
        same scene in seconds. Each file documents its own invocation - an XML
        comment cannot carry a double hyphen, so command lines cannot live here.
    -->
    <Canvas x:Name="Face" Width="640" Height="640" HorizontalAlignment="Center">

        <!-- ============================= MOVEMENT ============================= -->
        <!-- Painted first, because the dial goes OVER it: case.png has the
             opening punched clear through its alpha, so the movement shows
             through the hole rather than being pasted into a circle. -->

        <!-- Mainplate, jewels in their countersunk bores, and the floor beneath
             them. Everything that neither turns nor bridges. -->
        <Image Source="ms-appx:///Assets/movement-base.png" Width="640" Height="640" />

        <!-- The going-train wheel and the pinion under it, turning about 34
             seconds to the revolution. It is drawn IN MESH with the escape
             pinion, so it also turns the opposite way - see Caliber. Like the
             escape wheel it is LOCKED for 94% of every beat and runs only while
             the lever is crossing, because that is what the lever is for. -->
        <Image Source="ms-appx:///Assets/movement-train.png" Width="640" Height="640">
            <Image.RenderTransform><RotateTransform x:Name="TrainAngle" CenterX="333.8" CenterY="360.4" /></Image.RenderTransform>
        </Image>

        <!-- The escape wheel with its collet and its seven-leaf pinion. One
             image, because they are one assembly on one arbor. -->
        <Image Source="ms-appx:///Assets/movement-escape.png" Width="640" Height="640">
            <Image.RenderTransform><RotateTransform x:Name="EscapeAngle" CenterX="387.2" CenterY="391.2" /></Image.RenderTransform>
        </Image>

        <!-- The lever and its two ruby pallet stones. It does not bank on a
             schedule of its own: it is DRIVEN by the impulse pin, and can only
             move during the seven milliseconds a beat that the balance is close
             enough to centre to be inside the fork slot. Which is why it and
             the balance below share one number - see Caliber.Read. -->
        <Image Source="ms-appx:///Assets/movement-fork.png" Width="640" Height="640">
            <Image.RenderTransform><RotateTransform x:Name="ForkAngle" CenterX="356.4" CenterY="430.3" /></Image.RenderTransform>
        </Image>

        <!-- The hairspring. It turns with the balance staff it is pinned to,
             but only a FRACTION as far: the outer end is fixed to the cock, so
             the coils breathe rather than sweep. Turning it rigidly with the
             wheel would whip the stud round 570 degrees, which is the one thing
             about a hairspring everybody can see is wrong. -->
        <Image Source="ms-appx:///Assets/movement-spring.png" Width="640" Height="640">
            <Image.RenderTransform><RotateTransform x:Name="SpringAngle" CenterX="292.0" CenterY="466.8" /></Image.RenderTransform>
        </Image>

        <!--
            The balance and its timing screws, in one image so the screws cannot
            stay put while the wheel they are threaded into turns underneath
            them - and then the SAME wheel again, smeared.

            TWO COPIES, CROSS-FADED, because of the display and not the watch.
            This is the fastest thing in a watch: 285 degrees either side of
            centre, four times a second, which at 60fps is up to 119 degrees
            between consecutive frames. A two-armed wheel stepping 119 degrees
            is indistinguishable from one stepping 61 the other way, so a
            perfectly correct balance reads as a disc spinning at some huge
            random speed. That is the wagon-wheel effect and it is a sampling
            limit - there is no rate to correct, because the rate is right.

            A real balance answers it for us: it does not resolve into arms
            either. It smears into a translucent ring through the middle of the
            swing and snaps into focus at the two turning points, where it is
            momentarily stopped. So the sharp render and the smear trade places
            on the balance's own angular speed, and at the speeds that alias
            there is no detail left to alias. One RotateTransform over both,
            because they are the same wheel.
        -->
        <Canvas Width="640" Height="640">
            <Canvas.RenderTransform><RotateTransform x:Name="BalanceAngle" CenterX="292.0" CenterY="466.8" /></Canvas.RenderTransform>
            <Image x:Name="BalanceSharp" Source="ms-appx:///Assets/movement-balance.png" Width="640" Height="640" />
            <Image x:Name="BalanceBlur" Source="ms-appx:///Assets/movement-balance-blur.png" Width="640" Height="640" Opacity="0" />
        </Canvas>

        <!-- The cock, and the shadow it throws across the balance turning under
             it. Static, but it has to be painted here rather than into the
             plate: it is ABOVE the balance. -->
        <Image Source="ms-appx:///Assets/movement-cock.png" Width="640" Height="640" />

        <!-- =========================== DIAL AND CASE ========================== -->
        <!-- Bezel, dial, applied indices and the ring framing the opening, with
             the opening itself punched out of the alpha. The soleil finish and
             the printing are a texture from tools/dial_render.py; everything
             else here is modelled and lit. -->
        <Image Source="ms-appx:///Assets/case.png" Width="640" Height="640" />

        <!-- ============================== HANDS ============================== -->
        <!--
            Dauphine, faceted, and rendered rather than drawn - which needed one
            deliberate exception to this face's single-light-direction rule.

            A rendered hand is baked pointing at twelve and then TURNED. Under
            the face's key light in the upper left, the lit facet would turn
            with it, so at six o'clock the hand would appear lit from the lower
            right while every shadow on the dial still pointed the other way.
            Instead the hands are lit by a source centred on their own pivot
            axis, which is rotationally symmetric about it: turning the hand is
            then a SYMMETRY of the lighting, and one baked image is correct at
            every hour rather than only at the one it was rendered at.

            All of them pivot on (320,320), the centre of the face.
        -->
        <Image Source="ms-appx:///Assets/hand-hour.png" Width="640" Height="640">
            <Image.RenderTransform><RotateTransform x:Name="HourAngle" CenterX="320" CenterY="320" /></Image.RenderTransform>
        </Image>
        <Image Source="ms-appx:///Assets/hand-minute.png" Width="640" Height="640">
            <Image.RenderTransform><RotateTransform x:Name="MinuteAngle" CenterX="320" CenterY="320" /></Image.RenderTransform>
        </Image>

        <!-- Seconds: a gold needle with a pierced counterweight. It is the hand
             that carries the beat, so it is the one the eye should find first,
             and the only thing on the dial in a different metal. -->
        <Image Source="ms-appx:///Assets/hand-second.png" Width="640" Height="640">
            <Image.RenderTransform><RotateTransform x:Name="SecondAngle" CenterX="320" CenterY="320" /></Image.RenderTransform>
        </Image>

        <!-- The boss over the three stacked arbors. Painted last of everything,
             because that is what it is for. -->
        <Image Source="ms-appx:///Assets/hand-cap.png" Width="640" Height="640" />

    </Canvas>

</UserControl>
